NAME
       nfs - mount options for NFS shares

SYNOPSIS
       mount -t nfs [-o OPTIONS] SERVER:/PATH DIRECTORY
       SERVER:/PATH  DIRECTORY  nfs  OPTIONS  0 0     (in /etc/fstab)

DESCRIPTION
       NFS (Network File System) lets a client mount a directory that a
       server exports, and use it like a local file system. The client needs
       the nfs-utils package (installed on RHEL 9 Server). On RHEL 9 the
       type nfs tries NFS version 4.2 first and falls back to older versions;
       nfs4 asks for version 4 only.

       showmount -e SERVER lists what a server exports (for NFSv3 servers;
       with NFSv4 only you can mount SERVER:/ and look).

       Owners on NFS are numbers: a file owned by UID 1501 on the server
       belongs to whichever local account has UID 1501. Give users the same
       UIDs on all machines.

OPTIONS
       rw, ro Mount read-write (default) or read-only. The server's export
              can still refuse writes.

       vers=N, nfsvers=N
              The NFS version: 3, 4, 4.1 or 4.2.

       sync   Write data to the server before a write call returns (safer,
              slower).

       hard   Keep retrying while the server is unreachable (default):
              programs wait instead of getting errors.

       soft   Give up after a while and return an error.

       timeo=N, retrans=N
              Timeout in tenths of a second, and number of retries.

       sec=sys|krb5|krb5i|krb5p
              The security mode. sys (default) trusts the client's UIDs;
              the krb5 modes need Kerberos.

       _netdev
              In /etc/fstab: a network file system, mounted after the network
              is up (NFS is recognised by itself, but writing it does no
              harm).

       nofail In /etc/fstab: do not stop the boot when the server is down.

EXAMPLES
       Mount a share by hand, then look at it:

           mkdir -p /mnt/shared
           mount -t nfs nfs.lab.example.com:/exports/shared /mnt/shared
           df -h /mnt/shared

       Mount it at every boot, read-only, with NFS 4.2:

           echo 'nfs.lab.example.com:/exports/public /mnt/public nfs ro,vers=4.2 0 0' \
               >> /etc/fstab
           mkdir -p /mnt/public
           mount -a
           findmnt /mnt/public

SEE ALSO
       mount(8), fstab(5), auto.master(5), showmount(8), umount(8)
